The summary shows `Inference: MCMC` along with R-hat and ESS diagnostics. GCV, AIC, and BIC are
not defined for MCMC fits.

## Inspecting the MCMC result

The `mcmc_result` property returns an `MCMCResult` object with the full posterior sample and
convergence diagnostics:

::: {#d8b944a1 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
mr = model.mcmc_result

print(f"Samples shape: {mr.samples.shape}")  # (p, n_chains * n_samples)
print(f"R-hat (max): {mr.r_hat.max():.4f}")
print(f"ESS (min):   {mr.ess.min():.1f}")
print(f"Acceptance rate: {mr.acceptance_rate:.3f}")  # mean per-leaf α for NUTS
print(f"Mean tree depth: {mr.mean_tree_depth:.2f}")  # NUTS only
print(f"Divergences:     {mr.n_divergent}")
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
Samples shape: (10, 4000)
R-hat (max): 1.0005
ESS (min):   1331.1
Acceptance rate: 0.770
Mean tree depth: 7.75
Divergences:     0
```
:::
:::


`mcmc_result` is `None` if the model was fitted with any other method.

### R-hat and ESS

**R-hat** (the Gelman-Rubin statistic) compares within-chain and between-chain variance. Values
below 1.1 indicate that the chains have mixed well. Values above 1.1 suggest you need more
warmup iterations, more chains, or a smaller step size.

**ESS** (effective sample size) estimates how many independent draws the correlated MCMC output
is worth. An ESS ratio (ESS / total draws) above 0.05 is generally considered adequate.

::: {#58347c1c .cell execution_count=4}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import altair as alt

diag_data = [
    {"coefficient": i, "r_hat": float(mr.r_hat[i]), "ess": float(mr.ess[i])}
    for i in range(len(mr.r_hat))
]

alt.Chart({"values": diag_data}).mark_bar().encode(
    x=alt.X("coefficient:O", title="Coefficient index"),
    y=alt.Y("r_hat:Q", title="R-hat", scale=alt.Scale(domainMin=1.0)),
    color=alt.condition(
        alt.datum.r_hat > 1.1,
        alt.value("firebrick"),
        alt.value("steelblue"),
    ),
).properties(title="R-hat by coefficient (red = > 1.1)", width=500, height=250)
```

